„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙzThen said Jesus unto Peter, Put up thy sword into the sheath: the cup which my Father hath given me, shall I not drink it?¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book*§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses—„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙQThen the band and the captain and officers of the Jews took Jesus, and bound him,¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙpand led him away to Annas first; for he was father in law to Caiaphas, which was the high priest that same year.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙrNow Caiaphas was he, which gave counsel to the Jews, that it was expedient that one man should die for the people.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¥And Simon Peter followed Jesus, and so did another disciple: that disciple was known unto the high priest, and went in with Jesus into the palace of the high priest.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙªBut Peter stood at the door without. Then went out that other disciple, which was known unto the high priest, and spake unto her that kept the door, and brought in Peter.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙwThen saith the damsel that kept the door unto Peter, Art not thou also one of this man's disciples? He saith, I am not.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¤And the servants and officers stood there, who had made a fire of coals; for it was cold: and they warmed themselves: and Peter stood with them, and warmed himself.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book*§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses–„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙGThe high priest then asked Jesus of his disciples, and of his doctrine.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¦Jesus answered him, I spake openly to the world; I ever taught in the synagogue, and in the temple, whither the Jews always resort; and in secret have I said nothing.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙgWhy askest thou me? ask them which heard me, what I have said unto them: behold, they know what I said.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ’And when he had thus spoken, one of the officers which stood by struck Jesus with the palm of his hand, saying, Answerest thou the high priest so?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙfJesus answered him, If I have spoken evil, bear witness of the evil: but if well, why smitest thou me?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ;Now Annas had sent him bound unto Caiaphas the high priest.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book*§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‘And Simon Peter stood and warmed himself. They said therefore unto him, Art not thou also one of his disciples? He denied it, and said, I am not.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ„One of the servants of the high priest, being his kinsman whose ear Peter cut off, saith, Did not I see thee in the garden with him?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ7Peter then denied again: and immediately the cock crew.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book*§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse ¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÆThen led they Jesus from Caiaphas unto the hall of judgment: and it was early; and they themselves went not into the judgment hall, lest they should be defiled; but that they might eat the passover.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙTPilate then went out unto them, and said, What accusation bring ye against this man?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙkThey answered and said unto him, If he were not a malefactor, we would not have delivered him up unto thee.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ Then said Pilate unto them, Take ye him, and judge him according to your law. The Jews therefore said unto him, It is not lawful for us to put any man to death:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙathat the saying of Jesus might be fulfilled, which he spake, signifying what death he should die.¦layout ªlast_verse …¤book*§chapter«first_verse!ªlast_verse&¦verses–„¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙuThen Pilate entered into the judgment hall again, and called Jesus, and said unto him, Art thou the King of the Jews?¦layout ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙXJesus answered him, Sayest thou this thing of thyself, or did others tell it thee of me?¦layout ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙuPilate answered, Am I a Jew? Thine own nation and the chief priests have delivered thee unto me: what hast thou done?¦layout ªlast_verse#„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÂJesus answered, My kingdom is not of this world: if my kingdom were of this world, then would my servants fight, that I should not be delivered to the Jews: but now is my kingdom not from hence.¦layout ªlast_verse$„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÚPilate therefore said unto him, Art thou a king then? Jesus answered, Thou sayest that I am a king. To this end was I born, and for this cause came I into the world, that I should bear witness unto the truth. Every one that is of the truth heareth my voice.¦layout ªlast_verse%„¥verse&¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines’‚¤textÙ%Pilate saith unto him, What is truth?¦layout ‚¤textÙoAnd when he had said this, he went out again unto the Jews, and saith unto them, I find in him no fault at all.¦layout©paragraphªlast_verse&…¤book*§chapter«first_verse'ªlast_verse(¦verses’„¥verse'¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‰But ye have a custom, that I should release unto you one at the passover: will ye therefore that I release unto you the King of the Jews?¦layout ªlast_verse'„¥verse(¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙYThen cried they all again, saying, Not this man, but Barabbas. Now Barabbas was a robber.¦layout ªlast_verse(